nk  ukrainians are going to fight back but  this is going to have an impact on  americans even though it seems to be  really far away  so what can america and the  international community do we expect  sanctions later today sanctions were not  effective in 2014 when it came to russia  taking crimea what hope is there for  them today  well i don't believe the sanctions are  going to stop them from doing what their  plan is but i do think there has to be  if you don't pay a price for doing this  he's going to do more of it if you  listen to that speech yesterday the  argument he used  for why invading ukraine the old soviet  rules you could make that argument about  any of the baltic states who were all  who are now all members of nato and many  of those baltic states were actually  part of the you know the old russian  empire even longer than ukraine you  could use the same argument to take  those so uh he he's not going to stop  with ukraine if he's allowed he must pay  a big price for it but i also think  we've got to return for example to our  own domestic energy production as a  matter of national security emergency  now because russia and this disruption  that this is going to create is going to  increase oil prices and we're going to  feel it at the pump here in america and  that by the way will increase the prices  on everything because everything that we  buy and sell is transported by something  that's burning gas and diesel  yeah i wanted to pick up on that senator  because you just said it will have an  effect on america even though it's very  far away can you elaborate exactly what  you mean because i think when we're  sitting here in this country our main  concern is covet our main concern are  empty shelves and escalating prices can  you really break it down why this is  important and why we should be paying  attention and care about this  sure i'll focus on four things the first  as i said is the price of oil the price  of oil the barrel goes up to 110 115  that's we're going to feel that at the  pump okay the second thing is um these  are things people don't think about  ukraine is one of the world's largest  agricultural producers the fourth  largest i believe in wheat fifth largest  in corn that's going to raise global  food prices which eventually impact us  because you know when somebody gets cut  off of that they're now they become our  competitors in the global market for  food costs the third is something a lot  of people don't think about neon gas  ukraine is the world's leading producer  of neon gas which is critical to the  production of semiconductors ninety  percent of the neon gas used to make  semiconductors in america something  there's a shortage of already uh comes  from ukraine if that's disrupted or cut  off we're going to really struggle with  i remember back in 2014 when when that  invasion happened it really increased  the price of the semiconductors you can  see now where that's playing out in our  economy and even our by the way our  space industry this this could  potentially  the russia piece of it being cut off  from russian pieces we still depend on  russia for key components of many of our  commercial launches you could begin to  see launches this year scrapped things  that are critical to both national  security and also our commercial  viability senator you believe that a  full-scale invasion war is inevitable  you also tweeted yesterday weakness  always invites aggression so what should  the biden administration do if you  believe that a full-scale invasion is  happening  well they should have treated yesterday  as a full-scale invasion i hope that's  what they'll do today last night they  seemed to indicate that them moving into  areas controlled by separatists is just  based as public acknowledgement of what  already was true and so i didn't think  that was an appropriate response but i  think beyond that if you look at one of  the things that gave putin the belief  that now is 